<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apulia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Puglia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Apulia (Puglia) is the region in the south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> that forms the heel of the peninsula, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>. In antiquity it was home to the Iapygian peoples, the Daunians, Peucetians and Messapians, before Roman conquest. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it passed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and the Normans, whose Duchy of Apulia became part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. It remains a major producer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, wine and grain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apulia.jpg" alt="Apulia" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apulia_in_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 835 Aghlabid forces campaigned in Calabria and <b>Apulia</b>, taking Amantea, Taranto and Bari, but the destruction of a Muslim fleet off Ostia in 849 halted the advance, and Malta was conquered in 870.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Grain from <b>Apulia</b>, olive oil, wine, silk from Calabria and wool were its main products; the great seasonal migration of sheep between the Abruzzi mountains and the Apulian plain was organised by a royal customs office, the Dogana della mena delle pecore, founded under Alfonso V in 1447.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the eleventh century Norman adventurers led by the Hauteville family took <b>Apulia</b> and Calabria from the Byzantines and Lombard princes, and between 1061 and 1091 Count Roger I conquered Sicily from its Muslim emirs, taking Palermo in 1072 together with his brother Robert Guiscard.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Wheat, wine, olive oil and cotton were the chief products: 67 million litres of oil in 1844, mostly from <b>Apulia</b> and Calabria, and 191 million litres of wine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Outside Cerignola in <b>Apulia</b>, a Spanish force of around 9,000 men with 1,000 arquebusiers defeated a French army of 9,000, whose commander Louis d'Armagnac, Duke of Nemours, was killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-28">28 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Normans under Humphrey of Hauteville, Count of <b>Apulia</b>, defeated a Swabian-Italian-Lombard army organised by Pope Leo IX in southern Italy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-18">18 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
